1	//! mbox — a mailbox: many RFC 5322 messages concatenated in one file.
2	//!
3	//! Nobody has a folder of `.eml` files. What people actually hold is ONE very
4	//! large file: a Google Takeout export (`All mail Including Spam and
5	//! Trash.mbox`, routinely many GB), a Thunderbird folder, an Apple Mail
6	//! `mbox`, a mutt/Dovecot spool. This module splits that file into messages
7	//! and hands each one to [`super::eml::emit_message`] — the same code a
8	//! standalone `.eml` goes through — so the message and attachment records are
9	//! identical in shape, and only the locator is namespaced (`m{offset}-msg-s0`).
10	//!
11	//! ## The format, and the parts of it that bite
12	//!
13	//! A message starts at a *separator line* — `From <sender> <asctime date>` —
14	//! and runs to the next one. Because a body line can itself begin with
15	//! `From `, writers quote such lines: **mboxo** turns `From ` into `>From `,
16	//! **mboxrd** additionally turns `>From ` into `>>From ` so the quoting is
17	//! reversible. Reading undoes exactly one level: a line matching `^>+From `
18	//! loses its first `>`.
19	//!
20	//! The reference read for that rule is mail-parser's own
21	//! `mailbox::mbox::MessageIterator` (`mail-parser-0.11.9/src/mailbox/mbox.rs`,
22	//! Apache-2.0 OR MIT — already a dependency of this crate). Its unquoting is
23	//! at `mbox.rs:75-80` and ours is the same rule. It is NOT used here, and
24	//! nothing is copied from it, for four reasons that are each visible in that
25	//! file: it buffers every line whole with `read_until` (`:49`), so one
26	//! newline-free binary part is held in memory at once; its `Message` carries
27	//! no byte offset (`:21-25`), which is what our locators are made of; message
28	//! contents grow without a cap (`:70`, `:80`, `:82`); and ANY line starting
29	//! `From ` is a separator (`:55`) — see the next paragraph for why that one
30	//! is a correctness bug on real mailboxes rather than a style difference.
31	//!
32	//! A separator here must carry an asctime-shaped date (`Mon Jan  1 00:00:00
33	//! 2024`, optionally with a zone token, which is what Gmail Takeout writes).
34	//! That is deliberate: **mboxcl/mboxcl2** writers do not quote at all, so an
35	//! unquoted body paragraph opening "From what I understand…" is legal in those
36	//! files, and treating it as a separator would cut a message in half and index
37	//! the second half as a headerless ghost. Requiring the date shape costs
38	//! nothing on real separators — Takeout, Thunderbird (`From - Tue Oct …`),
39	//! Apple Mail, mutt, Dovecot and Python's `mailbox` all write it.
40	//!
41	//! ## Memory
42	//!
43	//! Multi-GB files are the normal case, so nothing here is proportional to the
44	//! file. The reader holds one buffered chunk, at most [`HEAD_CAP`] bytes of
45	//! the current line while it decides what the line is, and the ONE message
46	//! being assembled — itself capped at [`super::eml::MAX_EML`]. A line of any
47	//! length (binary `Content-Transfer-Encoding` has no line structure at all) is
48	//! streamed through in chunks; it is never materialised.
49	//!
50	//! ## Parallelism
51	//!
52	//! Phase B runs one worker per FILE, and a Takeout mailbox is one file, so
53	//! before this every message of a multi-GB export was extracted on ONE
54	//! thread — including one PDF parser subprocess per attached PDF — while the
55	//! other cores idled (measured: ~4.7 MB/s on the 1 GB synthetic Takeout, the
56	//! figures are in `benchmarks/mbox-ingest/README.md`). Splitting is cheap
57	//! and inherently serial; parsing is not. So the splitter runs on its own
58	//! thread and hands each message to a pool that runs [`emit_message`], and
59	//! the calling thread forwards the results to the sink IN MESSAGE ORDER, so
60	//! the record stream is byte-for-byte what the sequential path produces (a
61	//! test pins that). Memory stays bounded by [`IN_FLIGHT_BUDGET`], which is
62	//! process-wide like the slots below: a splitter waits before reading a
63	//! message that would push the bytes held across every mailbox's pool and
64	//! reorder buffer over it, and a single message
65	//! larger than the whole budget is admitted alone. Phase-A sampling (a byte
66	//! limit) stays sequential so that what gets sampled is a function of the
67	//! bytes, not of scheduling. Slots are process-wide (`configure_parallelism`,
68	//! set from `--workers`), so ten mailboxes in one tree share the cores
69	//! instead of each taking all of them.
70	//!
71	//! ## The record cap (#381)
72	//!
73	//! `MAX_RECORDS_PER_FILE` (4096) bounds what ONE DOCUMENT may emit, so that a
74	//! misclassified magic-less payload cannot become thousands of noise records.
75	//! It is applied here per MESSAGE — each body and each attachment is capped by
76	//! `emit_message` exactly as in a standalone `.eml` — and deliberately NOT to
77	//! the mbox as a whole: a mailbox is a container of documents, like a JSONL
78	//! file (which has no cap either), and a real one legitimately holds far more
79	//! than 4096 records. Capping the file would index the first few thousand
80	//! messages of a 100k-message mailbox and drop the rest. The threat the cap
81	//! exists for stays bounded: a payload that merely *opens* like an mbox and
82	//! contains no further separators is one message, and one message is capped.
83	//!
84	//! ## Panic safety
85	//!
86	//! This crate builds with `panic = "abort"`, and mailboxes are the definition
87	//! of untrusted input. Splitting works on BYTES throughout — a `&str` is never
88	//! indexed by byte offset, there is no `unwrap` on parsed input, and every
89	//! slice bound is derived from a `len()` or a `position()` of the same buffer.
90	
